course: everyday
lesson: 451
title: The check that settles it
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Ella's brother said, «Mum said I can have the last cake.» Ella said, «Let's ask her.» «Er, no — she's busy.» The person who is lying often doesn't want you to check. Ella asked anyway; Mum said «Share it.» Ruby's teacher showed the class three questions that settle almost any claim: How do you know? Can we check? What would prove it wrong? A two-second check beats a twenty-minute argument.
explain: Lots of things keep records. Kai's mate swore he'd beaten the final boss «in like ten minutes, bro». «Show us the trophy then.» The game keeps a list with dates — no trophy. He went red: «Okay, I watched someone do it on YouTube.» Kai laughed: «Fair, mate. Still counts as research.» Max's friend said the teacher said there was no homework; Max checked the class website — there was. Two lads in Leeds argued about who scored the winning goal in the playground. «It were me, you donut!» («It were» is Yorkshire for «it was».) A kid had filmed it on a tablet: it went in off Liam's knee, and everyone howled.
explain: News and messages can be checked too. Mia's uncle shared a post: «Schools to ban homework from next month!» Mia's mum checked the government website and the local news: nothing. The post came from a page called «LOL News Daily». If big news is true, lots of trusted places report it. Zoe's mum in Cardiff got a text: «Hi Mum, it's me, broke my phone, new number, can you send money?» She showed Zoe and laughed: «Your brother's in the living room, love.» The check is easy: ring the number you already know, or look in the next room.
explain: A check isn't only for catching liars. Ben's little sister said she'd already fed the cat. Ben looked: the bowl was full and fresh. «Nice one,» he said, and she beamed. Josh said he'd tidied the garage; Dad didn't argue, he just went and looked — tidy! «Champion, son» (in the North of England «champion» means excellent). Aroha's friend said she'd seen a famous singer in a supermarket in Auckland; the singer's posts showed she was in London that day. They laughed: «Must've been her twin, eh.» Her friend wasn't lying — just mistaken. Even honest people remember things wrongly; evidence settles what memory can't.
explain: So check calmly, not to catch someone, but to know. «Let's have a look» is the fairest check there is: no accusation, just eyes. It clears honest people as often as it catches dishonest ones. One boy used the three questions on his big brother at home, who didn't like it at all.
fact: Someone who refuses a simple check, like «let's ask Mum», may be hiding a lie.
fact: Three questions settle most claims: How do you know? Can we check? What would prove it wrong?
fact: Games, phones and apps keep records such as trophies, dates and history that can settle arguments.
fact: Official sources, like a class website or a notice board, are better than rumours.
fact: A video or photo of an event can settle what memory cannot.
fact: Honest people can remember wrongly, especially when they want something to be true.
fact: If big news is true, many trusted news places report it; if only one odd page does, it is probably fake.
fact: Scam texts sometimes pretend to be a child with a new phone asking for money; the check is to call the old number.
fact: A check can prove someone right as well as wrong.
fact: «Let's have a look» is a fair check because it accuses nobody.
fact: Sometimes a person isn't lying but mistaken, like someone who saw a lookalike.
fact: «It were» (Yorkshire) means «it was»; «donut» (UK) is a mild word for a silly person; «champion» (North England) means excellent.
exercise: What did Ella's brother say when she said «let's ask Mum»? | «Er, no, she's busy» | «Yes, let's ask her now» | «Mum's not home today» | «Ask Dad instead, he's here»
exercise: What did Mum really say about the last cake? | Share it | Ella can have it | Brother can have it | Nobody can have it
exercise: Which are the teacher's three questions? | How do you know? Can we check? What would prove it wrong? | Who are you? Where are you? Why are you? | What colour? What size? What price? | Are you sure? Really sure? Totally sure? Promise? Swear on it?
exercise: How did Kai check his mate's boss story? | He asked to see the trophy in the game's list | He asked his mate to swear on it in front of everyone | He beat the boss himself | He asked the game's makers
exercise: How did Max find out there was homework? | He checked the class website | He asked a friend's brother | He guessed from the day | He rang the head teacher
exercise: What settled the argument about the winning goal? | A video on a tablet | Who shouted loudest | A vote by the class | The teacher's guess
exercise: How did Mia's mum check the «no homework» post? | The government website and local news | By asking Mia's uncle where he heard it first | By counting the likes | By sharing it herself
exercise: What kind of page made the «no homework» post? | A joke page called «LOL News Daily» | The government's own page about schools and closures | A big national newspaper | The school's own page
exercise: Why did Zoe's mum laugh at the «Hi Mum» text? | Zoe's brother was in the next room | The text had a nice emoji at the end, like her son's | The number was her own | She had no children
exercise: What did Ben find when he checked the cat's bowl? | It was full and fresh — his sister was honest | It was empty — his sister had lied | The cat was eating his dinner | There was no bowl at all
exercise: What did Josh's dad do instead of arguing? | Went and looked at the garage | Called Josh a liar in front of everyone | Took away his pocket money until he told the truth | Asked the neighbours over the fence
exercise: Was Aroha's friend lying about seeing the singer? | No, she was mistaken — probably a lookalike | Yes, she made up the whole story | Yes, she wanted to be famous | No, the singer really was in Auckland, buying milk that day
exercise: Why can honest people remember things wrongly? | Memories bend toward what we want | Honest people have bad brains | Memories are always perfect | Only liars have memories
exercise: Why is «let's have a look» a fair check? | It accuses nobody, it just looks | It makes the liar cry | It takes a very long time, usually days or weeks | It is what police say
exercise: What does «It were me!» mean in Yorkshire? | It was me | It will be me | It wasn't me | Was it me?
exercise: Who liked the three questions least? | One boy's big brother at home | The whole class | Ruby's teacher, who sent a letter home | The school website
test: Bro: «mum said I can have the last Jaffa Cake». You: «let's ask». Bro: «nah, she's busy, innit». What does refusing the check suggest? | He may be lying | He's always right | Mum is definitely busy | You should give him two
test: Mate: «beat the final boss in 10 mins, no cap». How do you settle it fast? | Look at the game's trophy list | Ask him to say no cap again | Believe him, because he's your mate and mates don't lie | Argue for twenty minutes
test: Uncle shares: «BREAKING: schools ban homework!» from «LOL News Daily». What's wise? | Check trusted news first | Share it with everyone in the class | Stop doing homework at once | Believe it — it says breaking
test: Text: «hi mum new number broke my phone send £50 pls». What's the fastest check? | Ring the old number or look for your kid | Send £25 to be safe | Reply asking what happened and which hospital it is | Send it, it's your child
test: «It were me, you donut!» «Nah, it were me!» What would happen without the tablet video? | They'd keep arguing with no answer | The goal would score itself again | The teacher would know already | The ball would tell them
test: Aroha's mate: «saw that singer at Countdown!» (a supermarket in New Zealand). Singer was in London. What's kindest to say? | Must've been a lookalike, eh | You're a big fat liar and everyone knows it | Singers never shop | That's impossible, go away
test: Ben's little sis: «I fed the cat ages ago!» Ben checks: full bowl. What does this show? | Checking can prove someone right | Checking is only for liars | His sister must be lying | Cats feed themselves
test: Your friend says «checking means you don't trust people, it's rude». What's a good reply? | Checking calmly helps honest people too | Yes, never check anything, because checking is rude | Only check strangers | Only check grown-ups
test: Two kids remember a match differently. Neither is lying. What's the best explanation? | Memories bend toward what we want | One of them is a liar | Football is too fast to see | Both are lying on purpose
test: In a cartoon, the detective just stares at a kid and «knows» he's lying. What works better in real life? | Checking facts and records | Staring hard at people | Guessing from their face | Shouting until they confess
test: Dad looks in the garage. It's tidy. «Champion, son!» What does Dad mean? | Excellent | You won a race | You're a boxer | Go to bed now
exam: Identify the principal significance of a person's refusal to permit a simple verification of his claim. | It may indicate that the claim is untrue | It proves conclusively that the claim is true | It is of no significance whatsoever | It indicates that the verifier is at fault
exam: It is given that a sensational report appears solely upon a single page bearing a humorous title and upon no recognised news service. Select the response consistent with the established facts. | The report is probably false and should not be shared | The report is probably true and should be shared | The report's title proves its accuracy | Recognised news services suppress all genuine reports
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the function of verification? | It may establish that a person has spoken truthfully as well as falsely | It serves exclusively to expose falsehood | It invariably constitutes an accusation | It is unnecessary where memory is available
exam: Determine the most appropriate response to a text message from an unfamiliar number, purporting to be from one's child and requesting money. | To contact the child by the number already known | To transmit the money without delay | To transmit half the sum requested | To reply requesting further details of the emergency
